    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Changing Forests – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Changing Forests</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> I Explored the Ecological History Of...</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Give a speech on an academic topic. Interactive: Engage in negotiations on various topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can give a speech about deforestation and its impact on local ecology (e.g., "Deforestation has affected biodiversity in our area.")
            Can engage in role-play discussions about ecological changes in the local area and propose environmentally responsible actions (e.g., "If people planted more trees, the forests would recover faster.")
            Can participate in negotiations about environmental changes and ask follow-up questions to encourage detailed answers (e.g., "If we limited urban expansion, would local wildlife have more space to thrive?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students watch a short video (1-2 minutes) or view a series of contrasting images showing a healthy forest vs. a deforested area in Panama. In pairs, they discuss: "What are the most significant changes you observe in these forests?" and "How have these changes impacted local biodiversity?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models asking questions about environmental impact and discussing conservation ideas using target grammar and vocabulary. For example, "How has deforestation affected biodiversity in our area?" or "If we preserve our forests, we will help reduce plastic waste." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /aɪ/ in words like 'life' and 'diversity', and word stress in 'ecological' and 'dramatic'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'biodiversity' mean in your own words? Is it about variety or a single species?" (Checks understanding of 'biodiversity' and concept related to /aɪ/ sound).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'sustainable solutions,' what kind of actions am I referring to? Temporary or long-term?" (Checks understanding of 'sustainable' and relates to pragmatic discussions).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Changing Forests".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students listen to a short (2-3 minute) audio clip from an interview with a Panamanian environmental expert discussing the impacts of logging and urban expansion on local ecosystems and proposed conservation strategies. The audio explicitly uses present perfect, first/second conditionals, and comparatives, along with target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Authentic audio recording (interview excerpt) about forest conservation, covering genre (academic discussion), register (formal), and format (spoken interview). A transcript of the audio is provided to support comprehension without turning it into a reading task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in negotiations on various topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (for speaking preparation):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs to identify 3 main problems and 2 main solutions mentioned by the expert regarding "Our Changing Forests." (Gist questions, prepares for discussion).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students discuss with their partner whether they agree or disagree with the expert's statements about urban expansion having less biodiversity compared to natural habitats, providing reasons. (Encourages spoken interaction based on input).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Speaking.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Present perfect, first and second conditional, comparatives, and target vocabulary (deforestation, biodiversity, pollution, ecosystems, conservation, plant, reduce, improve, protect).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Reconstruction:</b> The teacher provides sentence fragments or scrambled sentences related to environmental impacts. In pairs, students orally reconstruct sentences, ensuring correct use of present perfect, first/second conditional, and comparatives. For example, "deforestation / significantly / affect / biodiversity / area" becomes "Deforestation has significantly affected biodiversity in our area." Or "if / plant / more trees / air quality / improve / faster" becomes "If we planted more trees, air quality would improve faster."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Chain Conversation:</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts a sentence about an environmental problem (e.g., "Plastic waste has accumulated in our rivers..."). The next student completes the sentence with a consequence or a conditional solution, using target vocabulary (e.g., "...and it has dramatically impacted marine life. If we recycle more, we could reduce pollution.") The chain continues, focusing on turn-taking and accurate sentence structure.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "What If...?" Dialogue:</b> Students receive prompt cards with environmental issues and potential actions (e.g., "issue: illegal logging; action: increase forest patrols" or "issue: reduced forest health; action: plant native species"). In pairs, they formulate questions and answers using first and second conditionals, and comparatives. Student A asks: "If we increased forest patrols, would illegal logging decrease?" Student B answers: "Yes, illegal logging would be less dramatic. Also, native species have better recovery rates compared to non-native ones." They then ask follow-up questions to encourage detailed answers, practicing polite expressions like "Protecting our forests benefits everyone."</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Changing Forests".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Role-play "Community Action Group Meeting."</b> Students will work in groups of 4-5. Each student takes on a role (e.g., Local Environmental Activist, Community Elder, Urban Development Representative, Local Farmer, Government Official). Their goal is to negotiate solutions for a specific environmental problem affecting their fictional local community, related to "Our Changing Forests" (e.g., rapid deforestation due to illegal logging and urban expansion). Groups must propose environmentally responsible actions and negotiate for their implementation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group is given a scenario briefing outlining the specific environmental issue.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students individually prepare arguments and proposals for their assigned role, focusing on using the present perfect (to describe past impacts), first/second conditionals (to propose solutions and discuss consequences), and comparatives (to weigh options or describe different impacts). They also plan to use polite expressions and community-focused language.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups conduct a 10-12 minute meeting, engaging in negotiations. Students must actively contribute, express environmental concerns politely (e.g., "Protecting our forests benefits everyone."), and ask follow-up questions to encourage detailed answers (e.g., "If we limited urban expansion, would local wildlife have more space to thrive?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At the end, each group presents a brief summary of their agreed-upon actions or the key points of their negotiation to the class.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Community Action Group Meeting" role-play. The teacher observes each group's negotiation and spoken interaction, marking against a rubric. Students also use a simplified checklist to provide feedback to their peers on specific speaking aspects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Rubric & Peer Checklist focus):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of words, especially long vowel /aɪ/ (life, biodiversity), reduced /ɪt/ (climate, approximate), and correct word stress in multisyllabic terms (ecological, dramatic).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Integration of at least 5-7 target nouns (deforestation, biodiversity, ecosystems, conservation), verbs (explore, impact, reduce, plant, protect), and adjectives (significant, sustainable, ecological).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammatical Accuracy:</b> Correct application of present perfect, first conditional, second conditional, and comparatives in spoken sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Fluency & Interaction:</b> Ability to sustain conversation, engage in negotiations, express concerns politely, and ask follow-up questions to encourage detailed answers without excessive hesitation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration & Role Adherence:</b> Active participation in group discussion and maintaining the assigned role's perspective.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (written):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new negotiation phrase or conditional sentence structure I used effectively today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did I express environmental concerns politely and use community-focused language during the role-play?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-reflection (oral discussion / quick write):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging aspect of negotiating solutions for 'Our Changing Forests' with different perspectives?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one specific way I can improve my speaking fluency or accuracy in future discussions about environmental topics?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? Did students actively engage in negotiations and use the target grammar and vocabulary effectively?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students to further improve their ability to give speeches or engage in complex negotiations? Perhaps more specific sentence starters for follow-up questions or mini-debates.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I ensure all students practice the target pronunciation points more consistently?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will focus on writing formal proposals or persuasive essays based on the negotiated solutions and ideas generated in this speaking lesson, further consolidating the vocabulary and grammatical structures. Students will formalize their "Community Action Group" proposals into written documents.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>